title:: Buffer Playback: Reverse, Channels, Buffer Bank
summary:: Comprehensive guide covering reverse playback, channel management, and buffer bank organization for PlayBuf and BufRd UGens.
categories:: Guides
related:: Classes/PlayBuf, Classes/BufRd, Classes/Buffer, Classes/Balance2, Classes/EnvGen, Classes/Done, Classes/SoundFile

description::
When working with sound files, you often encounter buffers with varying channel counts (mono, stereo, or multichannel).
This guide outlines recommended practices for:

list::

## Handling channel count mismatches between buffers and playback link::Classes/UGen::s (link::Classes/PlayBuf:: and link::Classes/BufRd::).


## Preparing buffers with specific channel layouts (selecting, duplicating, or reordering channels).
## Choosing the correct playback primitive (link::Classes/PlayBuf:: vs. link::Classes/BufRd::).
## Applying stereo panning and balancing efficiently.
## Reliably freeing Synths upon playback completion.
::

section:: Buffer Playback Basics

subsection:: PlayBuf vs. BufRd

link::Classes/PlayBuf:: is a high-level playback link::Classes/UGen::.
It handles triggers and looping internally and includes a code::doneAction:: argument.
Use it for standard one-shot or looped playback.

link::Classes/BufRd:: is a low-level link::Classes/UGen:: that requires an explicit phase (read index) signal (e.g., from link::Classes/Phasor::, link::Classes/LFSaw::, or link::Classes/Line::).
While it requires manual lifetime management, it offers precise control over playback position, making it ideal for granular synthesis, scrubbing, and phase modulation.

subsection:: Buffer.read vs. Buffer.readChannel

link::Classes/Buffer#*read:: loads the entire file, preserving its native channel count and order.
Use this method when you want to load audio as-is.

link::Classes/Buffer#*readChannel:: allows you to read specific channels, reorder them, or duplicate them into a new buffer.
This is useful for mono-to-stereo conversion at the buffer level (e.g., reading channel 0 into both channels of a stereo buffer).

note::
code::*readChannel:: creates a new buffer in memory.
Duplicating a mono channel into a stereo buffer consumes twice the memory of the original mono file.
::

section:: Preparing and Querying Buffers

code::
(
// Standard read
~b_ch1 = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.apollo11);
~b_ch2s = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.sinedPink);

// Read only channel 0 (Mono)
~b_mono = Buffer.readChannel(s, ExampleFiles.child, channels: [0]);

// Read channel 0 into both channels (Stereo)
~b_stereo_duped = Buffer.readChannel(s, ExampleFiles.child, channels: [0, 0]);
)

// Querying information
~b_ch1.query;       // Full report

~b_ch1.bufnum      // Buffer number
~b_ch1.numFrames   // Number of frames
~b_ch1.numChannels; // Number of channels
~b_ch1.sampleRate  // Sample rate

~b_ch1.duration;    // Duration in seconds
::

note::
list::
## In teletype::sclang::: code::seconds = frames / buffer.sampleRate::
## On the link::Classes/Server::: code::seconds = BufFrames.kr(bufnum) / BufSampleRate.kr(bufnum)::
::
::

section:: Strategies for Reverse Playback

subsection:: Using PlayBuf


To play in reverse, use a negative code::rate::.
To avoid stopping immediately when looping is off (code::loop: 0::), you must set the code::startPos:: near the end of the buffer.

link::Classes/PlayBuf::'s auguments code::loop:: and code::doneAction:: also affect buffer-boundary behavior and how the synth is released:

list::
## When code::doneAction:: is set to code::Done.none:: (teletype::doneAction:: number: code::0::), the synth does not free itself automatically when playback reaches a boundary.
## If code::loop:: is code::1:: (on), playback wraps at buffer boundaries and reverse playback continues indefinitely.
## If code::loop:: is code::0:: (off), code::startPos:: must be set near the end of the buffer;
otherwise, playback may stop immediately as it hits the "start" of the file.
::

list::
## If using code::Done.freeSelf:: (code::doneAction:: number: code::2::) you should choose a start position before the final frame so there is data to read when code::rate < 0::.
code::
(
{
	var buf = ~b_ch1;
	PlayBuf.ar(
		buf.numChannels,
		buf.bufnum,
		-1 * BufRateScale.kr(buf), // rate (reverse)
		1,                         // trigger
		BufFrames.kr(buf) - 2,     // start position (safe offset).
		0,                         // loop (off)
		Done.freeSelf              // doneAction: automatic freeing
	) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

note::
link::Classes/BufFrames#*kr:: is used to compute positions inside link::Classes/SynthDef::s at control rate. Its sclang-side equivalent is code::~b_ch1.numFrames - 2::.
::

## If you use code::Done.none::, you can start at the last frame without forcing an immediate doneAction:
code::
(
x = { PlayBuf.ar(
	~b_ch1.numChannels,
	~b_ch1.bufnum,
	-1 * BufRateScale.kr(~b_ch1), // rate (reverse)
	1,                            // trigger
	~b_ch1.numFrames - 1,         // startPos (or BufFrames.kr(~b_ch1) - 1)
	0,                            // loop
	Done.none                     // doneAction: no automatic freeing
) ! 2 }.play
)

x.free
::
::

subsection:: Using BufRd

With link::Classes/BufRd::, the code::phase:: argument directly controls the playback direction and speed.

list::
## Using link::Classes/Line:: (One-shot Reverse):

A simple way to play a buffer once in reverse:

code::
(
{
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(~b_ch1);
	var duration = frames / BufSampleRate.kr(~b_ch1);
	var phase = Line.ar(frames - 1, 0, duration, doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(~b_ch1.numChannels, ~b_ch1, phase) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

## Using link::Classes/Phasor:: (Continuous Looping):

When the rate is negative, link::Classes/Phasor:: moves from code::end:: to code::start::. To start at the end immediately, set code::resetPos:: to the end value.

code::
(
x = {
	var rate = -1;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(~b_ch1);
	var phase = Phasor.ar(
		0,                              // trig
		rate * BufRateScale.kr(~b_ch1), // rate
		0,                              // start
		frames - 1,                     // end
		frames - 1                      // resetPos
	);
	BufRd.ar(~b_ch1.numChannels, ~b_ch1, phase) ! 2
}.play;
)

x.free
::

With link::Classes/Line::, changing playback direction is complicated:

code::
(
{
	var rate = -1;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(~b_ch1);
	var start = if(rate >= 0) { 0 } { frames - 1 };
	var end = if(rate >= 0) { frames - 1 } { 0 };
	var duration = (frames - 1) / (SampleRate.ir * rate.abs * BufRateScale.kr(~b_ch1));
	var phase = Line.ar(start, end, duration, 1, 0, Done.freeSelf);
	BufRd.ar(
		~b_ch1.numChannels,
		~b_ch1.bufnum,
		phase,
		0
	) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

table::
## One-shot playback using link::Classes/Phasor:::
code::
(
{
	var rate = -1;
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(~b_ch1);
	var duration = (frames - 1) / (SampleRate.ir * rate.abs * BufRateScale.kr(~b_ch1));
	var phase = Phasor.ar(
		0,
		rate * BufRateScale.kr(~b_ch1),
		0,
		BufFrames.kr(~b_ch1) - 1
	);
	FreeSelfWhenDone.kr(Line.kr(0, 1, duration));
	BufRd.ar(~b_ch1.numChannels, ~b_ch1, phase,	0) ! 2
}.play;
)
::
::
::

section:: Handling Channel Count Mismatches

If the number of channels specified in code::PlayBuf.ar(numChannels, ...):: and code::BufRd.ar(numChannels, ...):: does not match the actual buffer, the server will post a message:
teletype::
Buffer link::Classes/UGen:: channel mismatch: expected 2, yet buffer has 1 channels
::

subsection:: Strategy 1: Dynamic SynthDef Generation

A common way is to create separate link::Classes/SynthDef::s for mono and stereo playback.
This approach is effective when you have a collection of both mono and stereo files and want to handle them with specific link::Classes/SynthDef::s, knowing the channel count of each file in advance.

subsubsection:: Using PlayBuf

numberedlist::
## Write link::Classes/SynthDef:: with iteration:
code::
(
[1, 2].do { |numCh|
	var name = (\playBuf_ ++ numCh ++ \chBuf).asSymbol;
	SynthDef(name, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, pos = 0, amp = 1|
		var sig = PlayBuf.ar(
			numCh,
			buf,
			rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf),
			trig,
			start,
			loop,
			Done.freeSelf
		);
		sig = if(numCh == 1) { sig ! 2 } { sig };
		sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp);
		Out.ar(out, sig);
	}).add
};
)
::
## Playback
code::
Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf:~b_ch1]);

Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf:~b_mono]);

Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [buf:~b_ch1, rate:2, pos:-1]);

x = Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, [\buf, ~b_ch2s, \rate, -1/2, \start, ~b_ch2s.numFrames - 2, \loop, 1, \pos, 1]);
x.free

x = Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [buf:~b_stereo_duped])
::
::

subsubsection:: Using BufRd

numberedlist::
## Write link::Classes/SynthDef:: with iteration:

code::
(
2.do { |i|
	var numCh = i + 1;
	var name = (\bufrd_ ++ numCh ++ \chBuf).asSymbol;
	SynthDef(name, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, dur = 2, pos = 0, amp = 1|
		var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
		var step = BufRateScale.kr(buf) * rate;
		var ph = Phasor.ar(0, step, 0, frames);
		var sig = BufRd.ar(numCh, buf, ph, 2, 0);
		Line.kr(0, 1, dur, 1, 0, Done.freeSelf);
		sig = if(numCh == 1) { sig ! 2 } { sig };
		sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp);
		Out.ar(out, sig);
	}).add
};
)
::

## Playback
code::
Synth(\bufrd_1chBuf, [\buf, ~b_ch1, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \amp, 0.6]);

Synth(\bufrd_1chBuf, [\buf, ~b_mono, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \amp, 0.6, \pos, -1]);

Synth(\bufrd_2chBuf, [\buf, ~b_ch2s, \rate, 1, \dur, 2, \amp, 0.8]);

Synth(\bufrd_2chBuf, [buf:~b_stereo_duped, rate:-2, dur:3, amp:0.2, \pos, 1]);
::
::

subsection:: Strategy 2: Using a Function Handling Synthdefs

subsubsection:: Using PlayBuf

code::
(
~playBuf = { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, trig = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var args = [
		out:out, buf:buf, rate:rate, trig:trig, start:start, loop:loop, pos:pos, amp:amp
	];
	if(buf.numChannels == 1) {
		Synth(\playBuf_1chBuf, args)
	} {
		Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, args)
	}
}
)

~playBuf.(buf:~b_ch1, rate:1)

~playBuf.(buf:~b_mono, rate:-1/2, start:~b_ch1.numFrames - 2, pos:-1)

~playBuf.(buf:~b_ch1, rate:-2, start:~b_ch1.numFrames - 2, pos:1)

x = Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [buf:~b_ch2s, loop:1])
x.free
::

subsubsection:: Using BufRd

code::
(
~bufRd = { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, dur = 2, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var args = [
		out:out, buf:buf, rate:rate, dur:dur, pos:pos, amp:amp
	];
	if(buf.numChannels == 1) {
		Synth(\bufrd_1chBuf, args)
	} {
		Synth(\bufrd_2chBuf, args)
	}
}
)

~bufRd.(buf:~b_ch1, rate:1, dur:4, amp:0.5)

~bufRd.(buf:~b_mono, rate:-2, dur:3, amp:0.2, pos:-1)

~bufRd.(buf:~b_ch2s, rate:-2, dur:3, amp:0.2)

~bufRd.(buf:~b_stereo_duped, rate:-2, dur:3, amp:0.2, pos:1)
::

subsection:: Strategy 3: Stereo balancing with Buffer.readChannel and Balance2

When the channel count of sound files is unknown,
you can force all buffers to stereo at load time using link::Classes/Buffer#*readChannel:: (by duplicating, selecting, or reordering channels as needed).
Instead of creating multiple SynthDefs, you can load buffers as stereo and use link::Classes/Balance2:: for consistent balancing.

list::
## For mono files:

duplicate the mono channel to create a two-channel (stereo) buffer.
## For stereo files:

use the buffer as-is (or convert only if a different mapping is required).
## For multichannel files (more than 2 channels):

read the first two channels, or explicitly select which two channels you need.
::

subsubsection:: Using PlayBuf

numberedlist::
## Write link::Classes/SynthDef:::
code::
(
SynthDef(\playbuf_balance2, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, start = 0, loop = 0, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(2, buf, rate * BufRateScale.kr(buf), 1, start, loop, Done.freeSelf);
	sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp);
	Out.ar(out, sig);
}).add
);
::
## Playback
code::
Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [\buf, ~b_mono, \rate, 2, \pos, -0.5]); // warning

Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [\buf, ~b_stereo_duped, \rate, 2, \pos, -0.5]); // no warning

Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [\buf, ~b_ch2s, \rate, 2, \pos, -0.5]); // no warning

(
Synth(\playbuf_balance2, [
	buf:~b_stereo_duped, // no warning
	rate:-1,
	start:~b_stereo_duped.numFrames - 2,
	// loop:1,
	pos: 0.5
]
);
)
::
::

subsubsection:: Using BufRd

numberedlist::
## Write link::Classes/SynthDef:::

code::
(
SynthDef(\bufRd_balance2, { |out = 0, buf, rate = 1, dur = 2, pos = 0, amp = 1|
	var frames = BufFrames.kr(buf);
	var step = BufRateScale.kr(buf) * rate;
	var ph = Phasor.ar(0, step, 0, frames);
	var sig = BufRd.ar(2, buf, ph, 2, 0);
	Line.kr(0, 1, dur, 1, 0, Done.freeSelf);
	sig = Balance2.ar(sig[0], sig[1], pos, amp);
	Out.ar(out, sig);
}).add
)
::

## Playback
code::
Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_ch1, \rate, 1, \dur, 4, \amp, 0.6]); // warning

Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_stereo_duped, \rate, 2, \dur, 3, \amp, 0.2]); // no warning

Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_ch2s, \rate, 1, \dur, 3, \amp, 0.8]); // no warning

Synth(\bufRd_balance2, [\buf, ~b_stereo_duped, \rate, -2, \dur, ~b_stereo_duped.duration / 2, \amp, 0.2]); // no warning
::
::

subsection:: Strategy 4: Downmixing Multichannel Buffers (Splay)


For buffers with more than 2 channels, use link::Classes/Splay:: to create a stereo downmix.

numberedlist::
## Preparing 8-channel sound file:

code::
(
var score;
~path = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "test.wav";

score = Score([
	[0.0, [\d_recv, SynthDef(\test, { |out = 0, amp=0.1|
		var freqs = Array.geom(16, 55, 1.48).scramble.reshape(8, 2);
		var env = Env.sine(5).kr(Done.freeSelf);
		var amps = { ExpRand(amp / 4 , amp) / 4 } ! 8;
		Out.ar(out, SinOsc.ar(freqs, 0, amps) * env);
	}).asBytes]],
	[0.0, [\s_new, \test, 1000, 0, 0]],
	[5.1, [\c_set, 0, 0]]
]);

score.recordNRT(
	outputFilePath: ~path,
	sampleRate: 44100,
	headerFormat: "WAV",
	sampleFormat: "int16",
	options: ServerOptions.new.numOutputBusChannels_(8)
);
)
::

## Allocating a 8-channel buffer from created file:

code::
~b_8chs = Buffer.read(s, ~path)
::

## Using link::Classes/PlayBuf:::

code::
(
{
	var sig = PlayBuf.ar(
		~b_8chs.numChannels,
		~b_8chs.bufnum,
		1 * BufRateScale.kr(~b_8chs),
		doneAction: Done.freeSelf
	);
	Splay.ar(sig) // Downmix 8 channels to stereo
}.play
)
::

## Using link::Classes/BufRd:::

code::
(
x = {
	var phase = Phasor.ar(
		0,
		1 * BufRateScale.kr(~b_8chs),
		0,
		BufFrames.kr(~b_8chs) - 1
	);
	var sig = BufRd.ar(~b_8chs.numChannels, ~b_8chs.bufnum, phase, 0);
	Splay.ar(sig) // Automatically spreads channels across stereo field
}.play;
)

x.free
::
::

section:: Managing Buffer Banks

To load entire directories of sounds into an link::Classes/Array:: instance, link::Classes/SoundFile#*collectIntoBuffers:: is highly efficient.
To load entire directories of sounds into an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: instance, there is currently no equivalent method, so the user must write code to achieve this.

To load hierarchical directories of sounds, recursive functions are effective.
This approach helps you load directories and subdirectories into an organized structure.

subsection:: Create buffer arrays in a directory

subsubsection:: Create buffer arrays with supported sound files in a directory

code::
(
var path    = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds";
~bufsSnd    = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
)

~bufsSnd.size // -> 3

~bufsSnd[0].play

~bufsSnd[1].play

~bufsSnd[2].play
::

subsubsection:: Create buffer arrays with specific sound file format in a directory

code::
(
var path    = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds";
~bufsAIF    = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*.aiff", s);
)

~bufsAIF.size // -> 2

~bufsAIF[0].play

~bufsAIF[1].play
::

code::
(
var path    = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds";
~bufsWAV    = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*.wav", s);
)

~bufsWAV.size // -> 1

~bufsWAV[0].play
::

subsection:: Create buffer bank in a directory and its subdirectories

subsubsection:: Prepare Files

code::
(
var baseDir = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
var sineDir = baseDir +/+ "sine";
var sawDir  = baseDir +/+ "triangle";
var renderNRT = { |defName, defFunc, outPath, dur, freq|
	var score;

	score = Score([
		[0.0, [\d_recv, SynthDef(defName, defFunc).asBytes]],
		[0.0, [\s_new, defName, 1000, 0, 0, \freq, freq, \dur, dur]],
		[dur + 0.1, [\c_set, 0, 0]]
	]);

	score.recordNRT(
		outputFilePath: outPath,
		sampleRate: 44100,
		headerFormat: "WAV",
		sampleFormat: "int16",
		options: ServerOptions.new.numOutputBusChannels_(2)
	);
};

[baseDir, sineDir, sawDir].do { |path|
	var file;
	path.mkdir;
	file = File((path +/+ "readme.txt").standardizePath, "w");
	file.write("test text file to see if errors occur due to non sound files.\n");
	file.close;
};

(60..71).do { |m|
	var freq = m.midicps;

	// 1) sine/percussive, 1 second
	renderNRT.(
		\sinPerc,
		{ |out = 0, amp=0.1, freq=440, dur=1|
			var env = EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.001, dur - 0.001, 1, curve: -4), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
			var sig = SinOsc.ar(freq) * amp * env;
			Out.ar(out, sig ! 2);
		},
		sineDir +/+ ("sPerc" ++ m ++ ".wav"),
		1.0,
		freq
	);

	// 2) saw/pad (sine envelope), 2 seconds
	renderNRT.(
		\triPad,
		{ |out = 0, amp=0.1, freq=440, dur=2|
			var env = EnvGen.kr(Env.sine(dur), doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
			var sig = LFTri.ar(freq) * amp * env;
			Out.ar(out, sig ! 2);
		},
		sawDir +/+ ("tPad" ++ m ++ ".wav"),
		2.0,
		freq
	);
}
)
::

subsection:: Create buffer arrays

subsubsection:: Create buffer arrays using Recursive Loading (Hierarchy)

code::
(
~loadBuffersToArray = { |path|
	var array = [];
	var files = SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);
	if(files.notEmpty) {
		array = array.add(files)
	};

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = array ++ ~loadBuffersToArray.(subpath);
		};
	};
	array
};
)

~bufferArrayHierarchy = ~loadBuffersToArray.(Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples")

~bufferArrayHierarchy[0] // -> [Buffer(...
~bufferArrayHierarchy[1] // -> [Buffer(...

fork { ~bufferArrayHierarchy[0].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } }

fork { ~bufferArrayHierarchy[1].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.1.wait } }
::

subsubsection:: Create buffer arrays using Recursive Loading (without Hierarchy)

code::
(
~makeBufferArray = { |path, array|
	if(array.isNil) { array = [] };

	array = array ++ SoundFile.collectIntoBuffers(path +/+ "*", s);

	(path +/+ "*").pathMatch.do { |subpath|
		if(File.type(subpath) == \directory) {
			array = ~makeBufferArray.(subpath, array);
		};
	};

	array
};
)

~bufferArray = ~makeBufferArray.(Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples")

~bufferArray.size // -> 24

fork { ~bufferArray[0..11].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } } // a chromatic ascending scale

fork { ~bufferArray[12..23].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } } // a chromatic ascending scale
::

subsection:: Recursive Loading into Dictionaries

Organizing buffers into an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: allows for descriptive access (e.g., code::~bufferDic[\sine][\note60]::).

This function loads sound files into link::Classes/Buffer::s and organizes them into an link::Classes/Event:: or link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: matching the directory structure.

code::
(
~loadBuffersToDict = { |path|
	var dict = IdentityDictionary.new;
	var entries = (path +/+ "*").pathMatch;

	entries.do { |subPath|
		var symName = subPath.basename.splitext[0].asSymbol;

		if (File.type(subPath) == \directory) {
			// If it's a directory: recursive call
			var subDict = ~loadBuffersToDict.(subPath);
			if (subDict.notEmpty) {
				dict[symName] = subDict;
			};
		} {
			// Filter by common audio extensions:
			if (["wav", "aif", "aiff", "flac"].includesEqual(subPath.extension.toLower)) {
				dict[symName] = Buffer.read(s, subPath);
			};
		};
	};
	dict
};
)

(
~rootPath = Platform.userHomeDir +/+ "Downloads" +/+ "soundSamples";
~bufferDic = ~loadBuffersToDict.(~rootPath);
)

~bufferDic.keys

~bufferDic[\sine].keys
~bufferDic[\sine][\sPerc60].play

~bufferDic[\triangle].keys
~bufferDic[\triangle][\tPad60].play

fork { ~bufferDic[\sine].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } } // not a chromatic ascending scale

fork { ~bufferDic[\triangle].do {|buffer| buffer.play; 0.2.wait } } // not a chromatic ascending scale
::

subsection:: Accessing Buffers: Array vs. IdentityDictionary

list::

## link::Classes/Array:: instances are best for sequential access or when filenames follow a strict numerical order (e.g., a chromatic scale).

## link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: instances are best for descriptive naming and organizing by category (e.g., teletype::\kick::, teletype::\snare::, teletype::\lead::).
::

code::
~invention_4_BWV775_motif = [0, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, -1, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 3]
::

code::
(
fork {
	~invention_4_BWV775_motif.do { |n|
		var offset = 1;
		~bufferArrayHierarchy[0][n + offset].play;
		~bufferArrayHierarchy[1][n + offset].play;
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::

link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: could be also convenient if the buffer names include MIDI note pitch number.
However, it is useful to access buffer with human-memorable names or example:

code::
(
fork {
	~invention_4_BWV775_motif.do { |pitch, index|
		var offset = 61;
		Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [
			buf: ~bufferDic[\sine][(\sPerc ++ (pitch + offset)).asSymbol],
			amp: 0.2
		]);
		Synth(\playBuf_2chBuf, [
			buf: ~bufferDic[\triangle][(\tPad ++ (pitch + offset)).asSymbol],
			amp: 0.1 / (index + 1)
		]);
		0.2.wait
	}
}
)
::
